  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nA mid level low will meander across E TX with weak high pressure \nover S TX through Thursday, keeping rain chances less than 10%. The \nmid level low is forecast to stretch across S TX beginning Friday, \nallowing slightly deeper moisture to advect across the northeastern \nportions of S TX. This will increase rain chances across the \nVictoria Crossroads up to 55%. By Saturday into Sunday, the mid \nlevel low is progged to be centered over the Coastal Plains. Rain \nchances increase farther south and west bringing a low (10-20%) to \nthe eastern Brush Country and southern Coastal Bend. Basically, \nmorning streamer showers and the afternoon sea breeze will be a \nlittle more active over the holiday weekend and into Monday. Models \nindicate more areal coverage of showers and thunderstorms on Tuesday \nwith low chances reaching the Rio Grande Plains and medium chances \nreaching the Brush Country. This is in response to PWATs increasing \nto 2.1-2.3 inches across S TX as the mid level flow becomes more \nsoutherly and taps into deeper gulf moisture.\n\nAnother Heat Advisory may be needed on Thursday due to similar \nconditions as today with heat indices up to 113 for a few hours. \nFriday through the weekend, the heat indices are forecast to be a \ncouple of degrees lower, but may still warrant Heat Advisories if it \nthe heat indices reach 110+ for more than a couple of hours. \nRegardless, there is a moderate risk of heat related impacts across \nthe Victoria Crossroads, to major risk across the inland Coastal \nPlains and, at times, extreme risk across the Brush Country on a \ndaily basis. Although areal coverage of the extreme and major risks \nare expected to shrink each day.",
